@prefix ag:   <http://example.org/agora#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix probe: <http://example.org/agora/moisture-probe#> .
@prefix wokwi: <http://example.org/agora/wokwi#> .
@prefix mc: <http://example.org/agora/microcontroller#> .

<http://example.org/agora/moisture-probe> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Capacitive soil moisture probe" ;
    rdfs:comment """One part, its own package. Three legs — an analog output, power and ground — and two calibration numbers that belong to the individual probe rather than to the model.""" .

probe:CapacitiveMoistureProbe a owl:Class ;
    mc:modelName "Capacitive Soil Moisture Sensor v2.0" ; rdfs:subClassOf mc:Peripheral ;
    # A CUSTOM CHIP rather than a catalogue part — the `chip-` prefix is the tell — so a Wokwi
    # project referencing it must also carry soil-moisture-sensor.chip.json and .chip.c. That is
    # a real constraint on anyone opening the generated diagram and it is said in the README
    # beside it, because a missing chip fails at load with nothing pointing at the cause.
    #
    # It was drawn as a potentiometer for a while, on my claim that Wokwi had no soil probe.
    # Wrong, and wrong in the way worth noticing: a plausible stand-in is harder to doubt than
    # a missing one, so it would have simulated perfectly well while misdescribing the bench.
    wokwi:part "chip-soil-moisture-sensor" ;
    wokwi:pin [ mc:pinRole mc:AnalogInPinRole ; wokwi:name "SIG" ] ,
              [ mc:pinRole mc:PowerPinRole    ; wokwi:name "VCC" ] ,
              [ mc:pinRole mc:GroundPinRole   ; wokwi:name "GND" ] ;
    rdfs:label "Capacitive soil moisture probe" ;
    rdfs:comment """Reports a raw ADC count that falls as the soil wets. A class rather than a model string because how you READ it is what differs; the model is for the person holding it.

Deliberately NOT a subclass of ag:Sensor, though every probe in this repository is one. The two typings are orthogonal and that is what makes them useful: ag:Sensor says what a thing is TO THE SOCIETY — polled by an agent, with a subject, an observed property and a reading topic — while this says what it physically IS. The same node in world.ttl carries both, from two directions.

Welding them would break the case the whole simulation rests on. A simulated sensor is an ag:Sensor with no peripheral class and no pins at all, and an agent must not be able to tell it from hardware; if being a probe implied being a sensor, the class would stop describing the part and start describing its employment. It would also make a spare probe lying on the bench — real, wired to nothing, polled by nobody — unstateable.""" .

probe:rawDry a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ; rdfs:label "raw when dry" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this probe reads in air. A fact about THIS probe in THIS pot — two identical probes differ — so it belongs to the instance, not to the firmware, which is per model." .
probe:rawWet a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ; rdfs:label "raw when wet" ;
    rdfs:comment "What it reads in saturated soil. Not free water: water is wetter than soil ever gets, so calibrating against it makes the top of the scale unreachable." .
